  # Project thread recap and follow-up

  Turn one or more team or project email threads into a structured recap — decisions, action items,
  open questions, and risks — then let the maker decide whether to post it to a Teams channel,
  schedule a follow-up meeting, do both, or neither.

  ## Required tools

  This skill needs three connected capabilities on the agent to run end to end:

  - An email tool (Outlook / Office 365 Outlook) to search and read messages in a thread.
  - A calendar tool (Outlook Calendar) to check for and create the follow-up meeting.
  - A Teams tool that can post a message to a standard channel selected by the maker, plus a
    directory lookup to tell internal participants from external ones when suggesting attendees.

  If any of these are missing from the agent's Tools list, tell the maker which capability is
  missing before attempting the step that needs it — do not guess at a result you cannot produce.

  ## Step 1 — Resolve the target thread

  Figure out which team or project thread (or threads) the maker means from whatever they gave you:

  - **Subject** — match on the words they used, e.g. "the thread about the Contoso renewal".
  - **Sender** — this is the default anchor when the maker names a person and nothing else, e.g.
    "catch me up on my thread with Priya Shah" resolves by sender first.
  - **Date / range** — "from last Tuesday", "this week".
  - **Topic** — keywords when there's no exact subject match.
  - **A link or message reference** the maker pasted in — resolve directly from it rather than
    re-searching.

  Search a **30-day window** by default when no date is given. If more than one thread matches, cap
  the candidate list at **5**, newest first.

  Decide single-thread vs. multi-thread mode from the maker's phrasing:

  - A singular qualifier — "the **latest**", "the **most recent**", "that thread" — means exactly
    one thread. Resolve to the single newest match and proceed without asking, unless nothing
    matches (then say so).
  - A plural or unqualified request with several matches — "my threads with the vendor this week",
    or a bare sender/topic that returns more than one thread — means multi-thread mode: list the
    (up to 5) candidates by subject, sender, and date, and either synthesize all of them (one recap
    each) when the maker clearly wants the whole set, or ask which one(s) when it's genuinely
    unclear.

  ## Step 2 — Produce the structured synthesis

  Pull every message in the resolved thread (not just the latest one) so decisions and commitments
  made earlier in the conversation aren't lost. For each resolved thread, write the recap in exactly
  this structure, in this order:

  1. **Summary** — two to four sentences on what the thread covered and where it landed.
  2. **Decisions** — one line per decision, phrased "what was decided — who decided it". Skip a
     decision if the thread never actually settled it; don't infer one from a proposal that got no
     reply.
  3. **Explicit Action Items** — one line per item, formatted exactly as "Task — Owner — Due". Use
     the due date or phrase as stated ("by Friday", "next sprint"); when none was given, write
     "No date given" rather than inventing one.
  4. **Implied Action Items** — only **first-person commitments** ("I'll send the updated deck",
     "I can take the follow-up with legal"). The owner is whoever wrote that sentence. Do not
     promote a third-person ask ("can you send the deck") into this section — that belongs in
     Explicit Action Items only if someone then confirmed they'd do it.
  5. **Open Questions** — anything raised and never answered in the thread.
  6. **Risks / Blockers** — anything flagged as blocking, at risk, delayed, or a concern.

  Keep every section header even when there's nothing to report — write "None noted." instead of
  omitting the section, since the maker and the later Teams counts both expect all six sections to
  be present.

  ## Step 3 — Confidentiality scan

  Before offering delivery, scan both the source thread and your synthesis for sensitive subject
  matter: compensation or pay, HR matters (performance, discipline, leave), legal (litigation, legal
  hold, contracts under dispute), security (incidents, vulnerabilities, credentials), medical or
  health information, or similarly sensitive topics.

  If anything is flagged, don't quote the sensitive detail back to the maker — just note in plain
  terms that the thread touches a sensitive topic. Do not offer or post a channel recap for flagged
  content. Carry the flag into Step 5b: any calendar invite for this thread must be marked **Private**
  and use a **generic title** ("Follow-up discussion") instead of the thread's real subject.

  ## Step 4 — Offer the delivery choice

  Show the maker the synthesis, then ask them to choose one: **Teams channel recap**, **Calendar
  follow-up (30 min)**, **Both**, or **Neither**. Do not default to posting or scheduling anything
  on their behalf.

  If they choose a Teams channel recap, ask them to select or confirm the team and channel. Resolve
  an ambiguous destination before proceeding. Only post to a standard channel; the Teams connector
  does not support posting to private channels. Show the destination and final recap, then obtain
  explicit confirmation to post. Never infer a destination from the thread participants or project
  name alone.

  Along with the question, suggest an attendee list for the possible follow-up: only participants
  who own at least one open action item, implied action item, or open question, **and** only
  internal participants — drop anyone whose email domain isn't the organization's own or whose
  directory lookup marks them as external/guest. Let the maker add or remove names before you
  schedule anything.

  ## Step 5a — Teams channel delivery

  If the maker picks Teams channel (or Both), use the Teams connector's chat-or-channel posting
  operation with **Post as: User** and **Post in: Channel**, targeting the confirmed standard team
  and channel. Post only after the maker confirms the destination and final recap. Include the full
  synthesis (all six sections, in order), followed by a count line, e.g. "2 decisions · 3 explicit
  action items · 1 implied action item · 1 open question · 0 risks". Do not post flagged sensitive
  content to a channel.

  ## Step 5b — Calendar delivery

  If the maker picks Calendar (or Both), schedule a **30-minute** follow-up:

  - **Attendees** — the confirmed internal list from Step 4.
  - **Time zones** — resolve every attendee's IANA time zone (e.g. "America/New_York",
    "Asia/Calcutta") individually; never assume UTC and never assume everyone shares the
    organizer's zone. If an attendee's zone genuinely can't be determined, ask the maker rather than
    guessing one.
  - **Working hours** — pick a start time that falls within roughly 9 AM–5 PM local time for
    **every** attendee, within the next 5 business days unless the maker asked for a different
    window. If no slot satisfies every zone, say so and offer the least-bad option (e.g. edge of
    working hours for the attendee furthest away) rather than silently picking a time that's the
    middle of the night for someone.
  - **Title and sensitivity** — use the thread's subject as the invite title, unless Step 3 flagged
    it, in which case use "Follow-up discussion" and mark the invite **Private**.
  - **Idempotency** — before creating the event, check the calendar for an existing event with the
    same title (the generic title when flagged, the thread subject otherwise). If one already
    exists, do not create a duplicate — tell the maker the follow-up is already on the calendar
    instead of double-booking it.

  ## Step 6 — Neither

  If the maker declines both, leave the synthesis as the final chat message and stop — no Teams
  post, no calendar event.

  ## Example

  Maker: "Catch me up on the latest Contoso launch thread and share the recap with the project team."

  - Resolution: singular qualifier ("latest") + project ("Contoso launch") → single-thread mode, no
    candidate list needed.
  - Synthesis: six sections, e.g. Decisions has "Renew Contoso for 12 months — decided by Priya
    Shah"; Explicit Action Items has "Send updated pricing sheet — Priya Shah — by Friday"; Implied
    Action Items has "Draft the amendment — Sushank (self, from \"I'll draft the amendment\")".
  - No sensitive topics flagged → normal title, normal sensitivity.
  - No sensitive topics flagged → ask which standard team channel to use, show the final recap, and
    wait for confirmation before posting.
  - Ask: Teams channel recap, Calendar follow-up, Both, or Neither. Suggest only internal attendees
    who own an open item, and let the maker confirm the list.
